{"A":"Own a conservative single-thread output-path study. Preserve the exact lexicographic sequence and formatting for every whitespace-stripped input, but target the obvious per-permutation I/O costs: eliminate endl flushing, disable unnecessary iostream/C-stdio synchronization if safe, and evaluate bounded batched serialization/writes rather than individual insertion operations. Measure variants and retain only verified gains; account for memory limits and empty input.","B":"Investigate scalable ordered parallel generation on the 4 ARM64 cores. Design only a general partition of the sorted distinct-permutation space into disjoint contiguous lexicographic ranges (including duplicate-symbol inputs), so concatenated output is byte-identical to the reference. Compare thread creation, synchronization, range construction, buffering, and ordered emission overhead against the small baseline; abandon parallelism if output serialization dominates or correctness proof/testing is weak.","C":"Own low-level sequential generation and formatting alternatives independent of A. Benchmark a compact specialized next-permutation/byte-buffer implementation versus the supplied generic iterator/template path, minimizing scans, iterator abstraction, allocation, and formatting work while retaining the initial sort, duplicate suppression, lexicographic order, and exact bracket/space/newline bytes. Seek general compiler-friendly layouts and validate edge cases such as all-equal, one-character, arbitrary bytes, and whitespace-only input.","G":"Integrate only measured, independently correct candidate programs from A/B/C. Rebuild and correctness-check combinations rather than assuming gains compose; choose the fastest valid whole program on the target. Guard exact output ordering/formatting, whitespace removal semantics, duplicate handling, and all valid input sizes. Prefer the best single approach when integrations regress from contention, buffering memory, or output bottlenecks.","analysis":"The supplied program is already algorithmically appropriate: sort once then enumerate distinct lexicographic permutations. With a 0.143 s valid baseline and output required for every permutation, likely cost centers are repeated formatted iostream operations and endl's per-line flush, not the permutation logic. The target has four cores, but output ordering makes naive parallel enumeration invalid or counterproductive. Research should therefore separate safe output/serial gains, a rigorously ordered parallel-range design, and compact generation/serialization; all candidates must remain general and byte-compatible rather than specialize to measured cases."}